00:03:32.000 --> 00:04:00.000
I think the current image always has to be lauded for its historic importance. It's a form of wayfinding, it's a form of easy navigation in the environment, and it's historically profound. I mean, it's politically so important that now you know you're guaranteed when you go out you're gonna find one of these spaces that's preserved. You're able to count on accommodation. But this isn't the end of the story because as the logo got absorbed into the built environment people with disabilities and their
我认为当前的图像因其历史重要性而始终值得赞扬。它是一种寻路方式，是环境中轻松导航的一种形式，并且具有深刻的历史意义。我的意思是，它在政治上如此重要，以至于现在你知道你出门时可以保证找到这些保留的空间之一。你能够指望得到便利。但这并不是故事的全部，因为随着这个标志被融入建成环境，残疾人及其……

8
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:23.000
allies started finding it kind of lacking. The shapes of the body and the body parts on that icon are squared off in geometric. And even though it does have a head. The head dangles from the neck, and the neck is in this kind of unnaturally erect posture. There's not a sense of organic shapes of the body in space. That's Sarah Hendren, by the way. My name is Sarah Hendren.
盟友们开始发现它有些不足。该图标上的身体形状和身体部位都是方形和几何形的。尽管它确实有一个头，但头从脖子上垂下来，而且脖子处于一种很不自然的直立姿势。空间中的身体并没有一种有机形态的感觉。顺便说一句，那是萨拉·亨德伦。我的名字是萨拉·亨德伦。

9
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:59.000
I'm an artist and design researcher in Cambridge, Massachusetts and I think a lot about prosthetics, adaptive technologies and disability studies and disability politics. Sarah Hendren is also a lecturer at RISD, the Rhode Island School of Design. And most importantly for our purposes, Sarah is the co-founder of the Accessible Icon Project. This movement, because that's what I think it has become, a movement, is made up of designers, activists, artists, academics, and disability advocates all trying to change the conversation about disability in their communities.
我是马萨诸塞州剑桥市的一名艺术家和设计研究员，我经常思考假肢、自适应技术、残疾研究和残疾政治。萨拉·亨德伦也是罗德岛设计学院（RISD）的讲师。对于我们的目的而言，最重要的是，萨拉是“无障碍图标项目”的联合创始人。这个运动——因为我认为它已经变成了这样，一场运动——由设计师、活动家、艺术家、学者和残疾倡导者组成，他们都在努力改变社区中关于残疾的对话。

