00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:31.000
He says he's seen the new logo and applauds the designers for the icon's dynamism. But he told me a lot of factors would have to be taken into account before the current symbol could be replaced. Like whether this new icon might be confused with symbols used for wheelchair racing. So as much good as the current logo has done, Sarah Hendren, among others, takes issue with how the logo portrays people with disabilities. It has this immobile look to it. The figure in the image looks like it's waiting to be pushed around.
他说他看过新标志，并对设计师赋予图标的活力表示赞赏。但他告诉我，在替换当前符号之前，必须考虑许多因素。比如这个新图标是否会与用于轮椅竞速的符号混淆。因此，尽管当前标志做了很多好事，但萨拉·亨德伦等人对该标志描绘残疾人的方式提出了异议。它有一种不动的外观。图像中的人物看起来像是在等待被推来推去。

13
00:06:31.000 --> 00:07:08.000
It's not that there's anything wrong with anyone who experiences mobility in that way. It's just that for Hendren, the implicit message the icon sense is that people with disabilities are helpless. In this new logo grants a sense of autonomy to the figure in the wheelchair. And there's a clear delineation between where the person starts and the chair begins. I think what the former icon lacked was that just wasn't any definition of moving forward. You know, it was just kind of, um, stoic. Whereas this symbol shows that the Maldon community is moving full steam ahead.
这并不是说以这种方式体验移动性的任何人有什么不对。只是对亨德伦来说，该图标传达的隐含信息是残疾人是无助的。这个新标志赋予了轮椅上的人一种自主感。并且对人的起点和椅子的起点有了清晰的划分。我认为前一个图标所缺乏的正是没有任何向前移动的定义。你知道，它只是一种，嗯，坚忍。而这个符号表明莫尔登社区正在全速前进。

14
